{:edb/db-before-t 842 :edb/db-after-t 843 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534155 50 #inst "2026-10-06T05:31:29.375Z" 13194139534155 true] [17592186105655 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279158 13194139534155 true] [17592186105655 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279165 13194139534155 true] [17592186105677 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279151 13194139534155 true] [17592186105677 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279172 13194139534155 true] [17592186106032 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534155 true] [17592186106032 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFind $∠QPR$ given the points $P(0, 4, 5)$, $Q(1, 5, 4)$, and $R(2, 3, 6)$." 13194139534155 false] [17592186106032 1100 "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(0,4,5)$, $Q(1,5,4)$, and $R(2,3,6).$" 13194139534155 true] [17592186106032 1145 "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨0,4,5⟩,\\,q=⟨1,5,4⟩,\\,r=⟨2,3,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,5,4⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279151 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279152 13194139534155 true] [17592186279151 1089 "q-101517" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279151 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534155 true] [17592186279151 1100 "Consider the points $A(2t,-1,5)$, $B(-3,t,1)$ and $C(2t+1,2,-t).$ Find $∠BAC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=5.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139534155 true] [17592186279151 1113 #uuid "840ed0f5-3459-5b4f-a6cd-56893ad1441d" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279151 1145 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩,\\,b=⟨-3,\\,t,\\,1⟩,\\,c=⟨2t+1,\\,2,\\,-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,t,\\,1⟩-⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3-2t,\\,t+1,\\,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨2t+1,\\,2,\\,-t⟩-⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,3,\\,-t-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=5$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =5 \\\\ (-3-2t)\\cdot 1+(t+1)\\cdot 3+-4\\cdot (-t-5) & =5 \\\\ -3-2t+3t+3+4t+20 & =5 \\\\ 5t+20 & =5 \\\\ 5t & =-15 \\\\ t & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-3-2(-3),(-3)+1,-4⟩, \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-4⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =1,\\,3,\\,-(-3)-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{406}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{406}})≈{75.63}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279153 13194139534155 true] [17592186279152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279154 13194139534155 true] [17592186279152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279155 13194139534155 true] [17592186279152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279156 13194139534155 true] [17592186279152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279157 13194139534155 true] [17592186279152 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534155 true] [17592186279152 1041 "selection" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279152 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279153 13194139534155 true] [17592186279152 1110 #uuid "15ab182e-bbfb-5624-8909-cb1e05f3c135" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279153 1034 "{75.63}^{∘}" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534155 true] [17592186279153 1111 #uuid "4b413d0d-adcc-5ede-88f1-f525f73eea61" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279154 1034 "{77.21}^{∘}" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279154 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534155 true] [17592186279154 1111 #uuid "e7607c45-5946-5e09-8e46-93b4cbfdf214" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279155 1034 "{79.84}^{∘}" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279155 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534155 true] [17592186279155 1111 #uuid "a371afc8-0ab5-56aa-9ecd-fcc72a15a54d" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279156 1034 "{73.42}^{∘}" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534155 true] [17592186279156 1111 #uuid "053f1e8f-6088-5c59-b0e7-80c34f51dd02" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279157 1034 "{71.36}^{∘}" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534155 true] [17592186279157 1111 #uuid "27f2d286-92ed-577b-a2e7-38b423d4a591" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279