tically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨1,2,4⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,6⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{40}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{170}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{170}})≈{58}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279167 13194139534155 true] [17592186279166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279168 13194139534155 true] [17592186279166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279169 13194139534155 true] [17592186279166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279170 13194139534155 true] [17592186279166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279171 13194139534155 true] [17592186279166 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534155 true] [17592186279166 1041 "selection" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279166 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279167 13194139534155 true] [17592186279166 1110 #uuid "e7d239a0-5555-5a5f-8e7c-5541a5a59980" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279167 1034 "{58}^{∘}" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534155 true] [17592186279167 1111 #uuid "696a450b-0b6f-5d21-b2df-f450197f9c10" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279168 1034 "{70}^{∘}" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534155 true] [17592186279168 1111 #uuid "7cc1a12e-1ec9-5610-8c5d-5006261870cb" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279169 1034 "-{85}^{∘}" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534155 true] [17592186279169 1111 #uuid "3e49d3ec-f0aa-5abd-9ded-ed8c9885adeb" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279170 1034 "{45}^{∘}" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534155 true] [17592186279170 1111 #uuid "bc13e402-5e5b-5248-82fd-f71f12f35d1d" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279171 1034 "{113}^{∘}" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534155 true] [17592186279171 1111 #uuid "b8ad0921-944f-5bea-ad24-07d4f0f5bfa3" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279172 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279173 13194139534155 true] [17592186279172 1089 "q-77165" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534155 true] [17592186279172 1100 "Consider the points $A(5,3t+2,2),$ $B(1,3t,2)$ and $C(1,4t,3).$ Find $∠ABC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}=4.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139534155 true] [17592186279172 1113 #uuid "e38d8e83-96eb-54fa-b9d4-c3057eacb534" 13194139534155 true] [17592186279172 1145 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨5,\\,3t+2,\\,2⟩,\\,b=⟨1,\\,3t,\\,2⟩,\\,c=⟨1,\\,4t,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠ABC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/2c81d48afacc4ac5e81ab501bc713009ea4425451fcf8dbf414672aeadbb1679.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{BC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =a-b \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,3t+2,\\,2⟩-⟨1,\\,3t,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =c-b \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,4t,\\,3⟩-⟨1,\\,3t,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,t,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,